1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,480
Section 4

2
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:07,940
Over the years, attitudes towards workers have changed considerably.

3
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:13,318
After all, there was a time when workers had no rights at all,

4
00:00:13,318 --> 00:00:17,150
and laboured in appalling conditions.

5
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:20,158
Conditions have improved a lot,

6
00:00:20,158 --> 00:00:24,329
but conflict in the workplace is still common.

7
00:00:24,329 --> 00:00:31,730
And human resources managers nowadays need to be able to deal with it when

8
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:32,270
necessary.

9
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:36,033
What is conflict in the workplace?

10
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:37,288
Definitions vary,

11
00:00:37,288 --> 00:00:46,069
but I'm taking it to refer to a whole range of behaviours that the

12
00:00:46,069 --> 00:00:54,850
victim finds unacceptable, from minor, harmless arguments to - at the opposite extreme - physical violence.

13
00:00:54,850 --> 00:01:02,468
Much of this is covered by the term bullying, by which I mean one

14
00:01:02,468 --> 00:01:10,086
or more people behaving abusively or aggressively against another who is in a weaker

15
00:01:10,086 --> 00:01:10,630
position.

16
00:01:10,630 --> 00:01:17,623
Although all behaviour like this is a form of conflict, not all conflict can

17
00:01:17,623 --> 00:01:20,120
be described in these terms.

18
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,208
As with all human behaviour, there are numerous reasons for it.

19
00:01:25,208 --> 00:01:31,684
But often it's caused by someone who feels the need to show their superiority

20
00:01:31,684 --> 00:01:39,547
over someone else, in order to feel that they aren't at the lowest level in a hierarchy

21
00:01:39,547 --> 00:01:41,860
or a group of people.

22
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:49,225
In some cases one person simply dislikes the other, on the basis that the

23
00:01:49,225 --> 00:01:56,590
personality of one is in some way incompatible with that of the other person.

24
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:04,311
A general habit of optimism in one person could make them intolerant of a

25
00:02:04,311 --> 00:02:11,480
colleague who's constantly pessimistic - not that that justifies treating them badly, of course.

26
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:17,326
Some conflicts arise when people are more interested in promoting themselves

27
00:02:17,326 --> 00:02:22,640
and their team than in the company as a whole.

28
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:25,400
These conflicts are called 'structural',

29
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,712
and could come about, for example,

30
00:02:28,712 --> 00:02:36,440
when a sales team believe they are the only people in the business who

31
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:38,648
do any useful work,

32
00:02:38,648 --> 00:02:41,960
and look down on behind-the-scenes administrators.

33
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:50,363
Conflict obviously affects the individuals concerned - the situation is likely to be very

34
00:02:50,363 --> 00:02:58,120
stressful for victims, resulting in their absence from work, possibly for months.

35
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:04,534
For the company, if no effort is made to deal with conflict, it can

36
00:03:04,534 --> 00:03:06,367
spiral out of control,

37
00:03:06,367 --> 00:03:10,490
and even lead to the breakdown of the business.

38
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:17,917
Some interesting work with chief executives - CEOs - has uncovered some of the

39
00:03:17,917 --> 00:03:22,250
reasons why they may treat colleagues badly.

40
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:25,995
Many CEOs combine two opposing characteristics:

41
00:03:25,995 --> 00:03:33,484
confidence - that is, the belief that they're capable of great achievements - with

42
00:03:33,484 --> 00:03:42,221
a high level of anxiety, a fear of missing targets, whether set by themselves

43
00:03:42,221 --> 00:03:46,590
or by the directors of the company.

44
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:54,530
This combination can make them respond badly to anyone who questions their decisions.

45
00:03:54,530 --> 00:04:01,500
In a high pressure work environment, such characteristics become problematic.

46
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:07,311
And it's particularly difficult to tackle the situation where colleagues, managers

47
00:04:07,311 --> 00:04:13,122
and board members are all trying to achieve their own visions.

48
00:04:13,122 --> 00:04:20,519
When they can't agree on strategic issues and on where they see the business

49
00:04:20,519 --> 00:04:23,160
going, there are real problems.

50
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:30,528
For managers at lower levels within the organisation, it might seem that an autocratic

51
00:04:30,528 --> 00:04:35,265
form of management - where the chief executive gives orders

52
00:04:35,265 --> 00:04:41,580
and everyone else has to obey - would see more conflict than others.

53
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:51,228
Interestingly, though, a company with a more democratic business model can suffer more when

54
00:04:51,228 --> 00:04:58,120
uncertainty about who to report to leads to conflicting demands.

55
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:05,321
Now I'll say a little about dealing with the type of conflict that has

56
00:05:05,321 --> 00:05:06,350
harmful effects.

57
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:12,614
Of course the ideal is to prevent it arising in the first place.

58
00:05:12,614 --> 00:05:19,360
A good manager, at any level, will make efforts to earn the respect of

59
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:24,660
the people they work with, particularly those who report to them.

60
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:28,287
That will involve politeness in all communications,

61
00:05:28,287 --> 00:05:35,542
and treating them as equals who happen to have a different role within the

62
00:05:35,542 --> 00:05:36,060
organisation.

63
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:39,633
Sometimes, of course, conflict does occur,

64
00:05:39,633 --> 00:05:43,205
and can get out of hand.

65
00:05:43,205 --> 00:05:49,160
In such cases the human resources department often gets involved.

66
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:56,590
However, if one of the parties in a conflict sees human resources as simply

67
00:05:56,590 --> 00:06:04,019
a mouthpiece for the chief executive, then an external mediator might be able to

68
00:06:04,019 --> 00:06:04,550
help.

69
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:07,393
By talking to both sides,

70
00:06:07,393 --> 00:06:15,354
and trying to find the truth of what's been happening, they can build a

71
00:06:15,354 --> 00:06:18,197
clear picture of the situation,

72
00:06:18,197 --> 00:06:25,020
and give feedback that both sides will accept, precisely because they're independent.
